In article <UZjy6U600VsV4H0mUc@andrew.cmu.edu> steve+@andrew.cmu.edu (Stephen Webster) writes:

>I like the potion-above-the-door-trap idea, especially if the player can set
>such traps on _open_ doors (kinda weird, but imagine that the door is only
>open a bit; you can't prop a bucket above a closed door, right?).  That way,
>you can pre-set a benificial potion trap on the way to doing battle with a
>bunch of nasties.  On the way out, with monsters close behind, you spring
>your own trap.

It works the other way too.. Maybe a closed door is just a little-little
bit open. You then could put potions over this closed door. Then anyone
opening the door would get the potion on his/her/its head. Hmm.. I think
I already suggested buckets filled with water for this effect.

Anyway I like more of the movable bear traps. With them you could stop
a horde of monsters at a doorway, but you could just as well (?) throw
the potion at a monster than set up a potion-over-the-door-trap. But I leave
it to your consideration what a bucketful of holy water would do to a wraith.

Another trap:
A rope is tied across a corridor and those monster who don't yet know 
about it stumble and get stunned. (Stunned is defined in the source as
being off balance, so it fits fine..) I assume that the walls of corridors
contain many sharp edges, so this is possible.
